The mindflaying attack is feared _only_ because it causes massive amounts
of tedious work to be done by the player. The effects can be counteracted
by casting ID for ages, redoing tedious price- and buc-identification, or
comparing with notes, in which case you did the work before the attack. All
of those cause little additional danger to the character, the main effect
is that a lot of time has to be spent. Thus, players treat (M)MFs as much
more dangerous than they actually are to the character. I agree that the
word "cheating" is a bit out of place, but my opinion is that by copying
the item identifications immediately before an (M)MF encounter, the player
circumvents a gameplay effect in a way not intended by the developers.

Wether making a monster scary by giving it the ability to make the player
suffer is good design or not is another matter, but that's the way (M)MFs
work right now.

> Taking good notes just sounds like "due diligence"
> to me, the difference in caring about
> winning/surviving between searching obsessively and
> thus never falling into a trap, versus blithely
> walking into every trap in your path depending on
> luck and hit points to keep you from expiring.

Taking those notes that the game doesn't take for you, yes. The game does
track item identities quite conveniently, the '\'-command has no other
purpose than to take some note-taking work off the player. MMF attacks
erase those notes, by design (good or bad). How could it not be a
circumvention of design intents to duplicate these notes as well?

